{{also|Firewall}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|fire|wall}}. Compare {{cog|fy|brânmuorre}}, {{cog|nl|brandmuur}}, {{cog|de|Brandmauer}}, {{m|de|Feuermauer}}, {{cog|sv|brandvägg}}, {{m|sv|brandmur}}, {{cog|is|eldvegg}}.  The verb sense for using maximum power or acceleration comes from the engine's firewall being the limit of forward movement of the throttle controls on many simpler vehicles; thus, to get the maximum engine output, one would push the throttle levers or pedal all the way to the firewall.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfaɪə.wɔəl/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfaɪ.əˌwɔːl/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfaɪ(ə)ɹˌwɔl/|/ˈfaɪ(ə)ɹˌwɑl/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-firewall.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-firewall.ogg|a=CA}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|architecture}} A [[fireproof]] [[barrier]] used to [[prevent]] the [[spread]] of [[fire]] between or through [[buildings]], structures, electrical [[substation]] transformers, or within an aircraft or vehicle.
#: {{cot|en|firestop|fire block}}
# {{lb|en|computer security}} The [[software]] that [[monitor]]s [[traffic]] in and out of a [[private]] [[network]] or a [[personal computer]] and [[allow]]s or [[block]]s such traffic depending on its [[perceive]]d [[threat]].
# {{lb|en|physics}} A hypothetical phenomenon where an observer falling into a [[black hole]] encounters high-energy [[quantum|quanta]] at or near the [[event horizon]].

